    "q-107184": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107184",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
